      "abstract": "This thesis endeavours to answer the question, \u201cWhat does the Reform Party\u2019s \u2018populism\u2019 consist of?\u201d An attempt is made herein to characterize the nature of the Reform Party\u2019s populism via Margaret Canovan\u2019s typology of populisms. The analysis concludes that the Reform Party manifests the characteristics of two of the seven different kinds of populist phenomena that Canovan identifies. It is found, on the one hand, that through his \u201cantipolitical\u201d rhetorical orientation, Reform Party leader Preston Manning evinces a certain form of what Canovan refers to as \u201cpoliticians\u2019 populism.\u201d On the other hand, it is posited that the party\u2019s policies vis a vis federal bilingualism, multiculturalism and immigration programs reflect Canovan\u2019s conception of \u201creactionary populism.\u201d",
